# Line endings, decided here rather than by each teammate's Git config.
#
# Git on Windows defaults to core.autocrlf=true, which rewrites LF to CRLF on
# checkout. That is harmless for source we only ever read, and breaks anything
# a Linux container executes: a shell script with CRLF fails as
# `/bin/sh^M: bad interpreter`, and the error names a file that looks fine in
# an editor. The team is on both macOS and Windows and the containers are
# Linux, so the files below are pinned to LF everywhere.

# Sensible default: Git decides what is text, and normalises it in the repo.
* text=auto

# Executed inside Linux containers or by bash — must stay LF whatever the host.
*.sh            text eol=lf
Dockerfile      text eol=lf
*.Dockerfile    text eol=lf
Caddyfile       text eol=lf
*.yml           text eol=lf
*.yaml          text eol=lf
.env.example    text eol=lf

# Binary: never normalise, never diff as text.
*.png           binary
*.jpg           binary
*.jpeg          binary
*.webp          binary
*.pdf           binary
*.zip           binary
*.ico           binary
# Office files are ZIP archives. `* text=auto` above would happily "normalise"
# one Git mis-sniffed as text and hand back a file PowerPoint refuses to open.
*.pptx          binary
*.potx          binary
*.docx          binary
*.xlsx          binary
